{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>Rejoice, Zebulun, in your going out.</b> That is, to war. And even though Issachar is the elder, for he was born first, he placed Zebulun before Issachar because he is the cause of Issachar's Torah. And so Jacob too placed him first in the blessing of the tribes, because he is the one who sustains Issachar's Torah, and were it not for his sustaining him it would be impossible for him to engage in Torah. Therefore he is worthy to inherit joy and honor in the joy of Torah in the World to Come — and this is what it says: ״rejoice, Zebulun, in your going out.״ And although the plain sense of ״in your going out״ is to war, it is possible to say: in your going out from this world, and he blessed him with the joy of the World to Come. And he attributed the joy to Zebulun because he is the cause of Torah, and this is what the Rabbis, of blessed memory, said: ״whoever places merchandise in the purse of a Torah scholar merits and sits in the academy on high, as it is said״ (Ecclesiastes 7:12): ״כי בצל החכמה בצל הכסף.״ And its explanation: the emanation of wisdom is sustained from the emanation of silver, and the emanation of silver is emanated from the emanation of wisdom." "<b>And you will find</b> in this blessing three things: the first, that he placed Zebulun before Issachar; the second, that he included the blessing of Issachar within the blessing of Zebulun; and the third, that he lengthened the blessing of Zebulun and said of him ״כי שפע ימים יינקו ושפוני טמוני חול,״ but was brief in the blessing of Issachar. And you will not find among any of all the tribes that Moses was as brief in his blessing as with Issachar, though it would have been fitting to lengthen it more, for there is no tribe as great in Torah as he, as it is said (1 Chronicles 12:33): ״ומבני יששכר יודעי בינה לעתים.״ And the reason he was brief in his blessing, saying only ״ואמר ליששכר באהליך,״ is that the blessing with which he blessed him in this word embraces all blessings. For what he said — ״באהליך,״ in your tents — refers to two tents: the tent above and the tent below. If so, he blessed him with the joy of the tent below, which is as written (Psalms 19:9): ״פקודי ה' ישרים משמחי לב,״ and with the joy of the tent above, where the joy is complete — in the language of the blessing that the Rabbis, of blessed memory, established: ״who has the joy in His dwelling,״ and there the reward is doubled. And perhaps for this reason the שי״ן was doubled in the name יששכר, to indicate two portions of reward: one for the one who engages in Torah and one for the one who sustains the Torah in his hand."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:54:20.696063139Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}